## Package comparison

The phase package remains aligned with approved Phase 1. The round-2 rework did not broaden scope, but it also did not address the current round-1 findings; the coder result says only `server.ts` commentary/bridge code was touched and explicitly acknowledges missing acceptance coverage and accept-queue ownership.

## Findings

### Critical — Provider restart supervision remains absent

`session/provider.ts:61-75` still acquires exactly one `subscribeAttempt` stream and contains no retry `Schedule` or reacquisition loop. A terminal callback queues an invalidation but leaves the callback stream open forever, so no second media-control attempt starts and daemon media events stop permanently after the first termination.

The one-attempt seam also remains non-exact-once: `system-media.ts:430-439` disposes the source on terminal without marking the attempt disposed. Scope cleanup can dispose it again, and late `onLine` callbacks remain eligible to publish after terminal.

### Critical — Connection handlers remain detached root fibers

`server.ts:252-259` and `:278-286` still call `Effect.runFork` from Node callbacks. Providing the captured server `Scope` does not supervise those root fibers. Each connection waits forever at `server.ts:266`, and natural socket closure does not close a per-connection scope; status/state forwarding fibers and listener resources therefore survive until whole-server shutdown. The immediately shut-down, unused `accepted` queue does not provide an Effect-owned accept lane.

### High — Polling still does not reset from authoritative state

`coordinator.ts:272-281` chooses one delay from a snapshot, sleeps, samples, and repeats with an unrelated one-millisecond schedule. Because the poll fiber starts before initial sampling, it can retain an eight-second idle deadline after initialization accepts a playing state. Later snapshots and commands likewise cannot replace an in-progress deadline, so the required resettable 3/5/8-second policy is not implemented.

### High — Interruption and defects are still mislabeled as provider failures

Sampling and transport still use `Effect.exit` (`coordinator.ts:138-143` and `:206-218`) and treat every failed cause as an ordinary provider failure. Interruption or a defect during blocked work can therefore degrade status or settle a command as `PROVIDER_FAILURE` instead of preserving interruption/defect and allowing shutdown to settle work as disposed.

### High — Verification still does not prove Phase 1 acceptance

No tasked test imports or uses `TestClock`. `session-coordinator.test.ts` still has one test, `session-server.test.ts` has three basic tests, and `system-media.test.ts` does not cover the new one-attempt seam or a daemon retry schedule. The passing 33-test transcript therefore provides no deterministic evidence for retry/reset, 3/5/8 polling, reconciliation timing, stale/coalesced sampling, saturation/failure continuation, blocked-work interruption, or exact-once provider/connection cleanup. The coder result explicitly acknowledges this gap.

### Medium — Stable command errors still regress at the server boundary

`server.ts:240-249` still maps every failed coordinator submission to `PROVIDER_FAILURE`, discarding tagged `SERVER_BUSY` and `DISPOSED` codes that the phase requires the unchanged socket boundary to preserve.
